course: dictionary
lesson: 15983
title: Uproot: pull out, move away, or get rid of
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Uproot is made of up and root. A root is the part of a plant that grows down into the soil, so to uproot is to pull something out of the ground along with its roots. «The storm uprooted two old trees in the park.» The trees were pulled out of the ground, roots and all.
explain: Writers also use uproot for people. «The war uprooted many families, and they had to leave their homes.» Nobody is pulling a plant here. The clues are families and leave their homes, so uproot means to force people to move away from where they live.
explain: You can also uproot a habit or a problem, which means getting rid of it completely. «We must uproot the weeds before they spread.» Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence. Getting rid of the weeds completely makes sense, so your meaning is right.
fact: Uproot means to pull something out of the ground along with its roots.
fact: «The storm uprooted the tree.» means the storm pulled the tree out of the ground.
fact: «The war uprooted many families.» means the war forced families to leave their homes.
fact: Uproot can also mean to get rid of something completely, such as a bad habit.
fact: Uproot is a verb: uproot, uproots, uprooted, uprooting.
exercise: «The storm uprooted two old trees in the park.» What does uprooted mean here? | Watered every day | Cut into small pieces | *Pulled out of the ground with their roots | Painted bright green
exercise: «The war uprooted many families.» What happened to the families? | They won a prize for their garden | They were given a bigger house | They planted a new garden together | *They were forced to leave their homes
exercise: Jamal wants to uproot his habit of biting his nails. What does uproot mean here? | Make it grow bigger | *Get rid of it completely | Do it again and again | Show it to everyone
test: «The gardener uprooted the dandelions.» What did the gardener do? | Watered them carefully | Painted them yellow | *Pulled them out of the ground with their roots | Counted them one by one
test: Which word is the past tense of uproot? | uprooting | uproots | *uprooted | uproot
exam: «After the flood, the farmers were uprooted from their land.» What does uprooted mean here? | Painted their houses | Given a new tractor | *Forced to leave their home | Taught to swim
